WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:04.811
The dust has settled, the bad guys have been
defeated, and all is right with the Marvel

00:00:04.811 --> 00:00:09.750
Cinematic Universe now that Spider-Man: Homecoming
has finally made Peter Parker an official

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.290
part of the MCU.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.490
But while Homecoming answered some questions,
it posed a few more — especially with the

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:16.180
way it ended.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.800
Here's a spoiler-filled explanation of just
what the ending of Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:21.300
means.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:22.960
The press conference

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.480
Peter turning down Tony Stark's offer of membership
in the Avengers was a bit of a surprise, but

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.700
it also had some hidden meaning — not to
mention implications for future films.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.970
Fans of the comics probably recognized that
the press conference sequence closely mirrored

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:41.250
a similar event in the comic book version
of Civil War, where Peter joins Iron Man's

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.000
side and reveals his secret identity to the
world.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.900
Luckily, in the movie, Peter turned down Stark's
offer, because in the comics, it eventually

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.690
led to a deal with the devil.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:52.120
Dodged a bullet there, Spidey!

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:57.680
More importantly for film fans, Peter's non-membership
in the Avengers sets up some interesting questions

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.320
heading into 2018's Avengers: Infinity War.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.100
Just what will Spidey's role be in the movie
if he's not a member of the team?

00:01:04.119 --> 00:01:07.700
Will he have a central role, or just be relegated
to a cameo?

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.320
Whether he's on the team or not, here's hoping
we get to see a lot more of Tom Holland's

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.780
Spider-Man in the future.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.500
Peter's love life

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.780
At the end of the film, Peter's love interest
Liz moved to Oregon.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:23.350
But more importantly for fans, we learn that
his acerbic classmate Michelle is actually

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:28.320
the MCU version of Mary Jane Watson, who has
traditionally been Spider-Man's true love.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:32.880
Considering there's no sign yet of Gwen Stacy
in the MCU, it seems as though Marvel may

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.260
be skipping Gwen's tragic death in the same
way they decided to skip Uncle Ben's death.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.820
"(crying)"

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.200
Boring!

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.040
Seeing how Peter's relationship with MJ develops
in future films should be interesting.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:51.740
The guy in the chair

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.500
One of the coolest parts of Spider-Man: Homecoming
was Peter's relationship with his awesomely

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.530
nerdy best bro, Ned Leeds, a.k.a. his "guy
in the chair."

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:05.060
One thing, though: he might also become one
of Spider-Man's deadliest supervillains.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.500
That's because in the comics, Ned Leeds eventually
becomes a Hobgoblin.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.960
And if that plot sounds a little too close
to the stories in the previous Spider-Man

00:02:12.969 --> 00:02:17.109
trilogies, where Peter's best friend Harry
became the Green Goblin, there's good reason:

00:02:17.109 --> 00:02:21.379
in the comics, Hobgoblin was born when Ned
Leeds found a stash of the deceased Green

00:02:21.379 --> 00:02:23.540
Goblin's weapons and gizmos.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.040
Of course, comics being comics, that was later
retroactively changed.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.080
But will Ned do a heel-turn in the movies
and become Peter's mortal foe?

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.180
We sure hope not, but we wouldn't put anything
past Marvel.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.900
Those other supervillains

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.440
While the Vulture ended up in prison, it was
less clear what happened to the film's other

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:42.849
supervillains, the Shocker and the Tinkerer.

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:46.529
But chances are pretty good we'll be seeing
them in future films, especially since the

00:02:46.529 --> 00:02:51.109
mid-credits teaser hinted at the creation
of the supvervillain team known as the Sinister

00:02:51.109 --> 00:02:54.840
Six, which in the past has included both Vulture
and the Shocker.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.129
And with the Tinkerer still out there making
supervillain suits, introducing other members

00:02:59.129 --> 00:03:02.580
of the Sinister Six like the Scorpion and
the Beetle should be easy.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.440
What's the big deal with the plane?

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.360
Considering the big lecture Tony Stark gave
Peter about the recklessness of starting a

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:13.930
fight with the Vulture on the Staten Island
Ferry, it seemed even more dangerously reckless

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.650
when Peter nearly crashed a plane into a Brooklyn
neighborhood just to stop Vulture from making

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:21.069
off with some of Tony Stark's leftover junk.

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:22.360
Didn't Spidey learn anything?

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.420
"Remember, with great power comes great responsibility."

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:32.120
But there are two pretty big reasons why Peter
actually did the right thing: the Tesseract

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.120
and Ultron.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.590
In both Avengers and Avengers 2: Age of Ultron,
the Earth was nearly destroyed when the good

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.060
guys lost control of massively powerful goodies
they were safeguarding.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.900
There's no telling what might have happened
if Vulture had been able to sell stuff like

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.159
the Hulkbuster armor and Thor's magic belt
to psychopathic supervillains.

00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:53.171
So even though Peter endangered thousands
of lives to save an unmanned plane, he may

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.260
have actually saved millions of innocent people
in the process.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.420
The X-Men and the Fantastic Four

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.839
With Homecoming, Marvel proved it can make
better Spider-Man movies than Sony.

00:04:02.839 --> 00:04:06.930
So imagine what they could do if they teamed
up with Fox to make a Fantastic Four movie?

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.090
The success of Homecoming may theoretically
make that possible.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:15.439
Right now, Fox isn't making a dime from Fantastic
Four or other related properties like Silver

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:15.940
Surfer.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:21.380
Even splitting the proceeds with Marvel, they'd
make a lot more cash co-producing an MCU Fantastic

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:23.599
Four film than they ever will on their own.

00:04:23.599 --> 00:04:28.060
That could spur Fox and Marvel to bury the
hatchet and bring the F.F. — and possibly

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.949
even the X-Men — into the MCU fold.

00:04:30.949 --> 00:04:35.270
And if Spider-Man can help reunite all of
Marvel's heroes in one film universe, that

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.580
would be his greatest feat of heroism yet.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.180
Thanks for watching!

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:41.979
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:41.979 --> 00:04:44.270
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

